<panel>
  <name> DopamineGlossary </name>
  <tablabel> Help & Glossary </tablabel>

  <showpanelname><row> 0.5 </row><col> 1.0 </col></showpanelname>

  <label>
    <row> 0.5 </row><col> 1.0 </col>
    <text> Help & Glossary </text>
    <color> BLACK </color>
  </label>

  <!-- ==================== KEY CONCEPTS ==================== -->
  <textbox>
    <row> 2.0 </row><col> 1.0 </col>
    <high> 35.0 </high><wide> 115.0 </wide>

    <text> 
      
=== DOPAMINE: THE MOTIVATION MOLECULE ===

Dopamine is a neurotransmitter (brain chemical) that drives motivation, 
reward, and craving. It's central to addiction because:

  • REWARD: Dopamine spikes when we experience something good (eating, 
    winning, or in chronic alcoholism, the relief of drinking).
    
  • PREDICTION: The brain learns to anticipate rewards. Dopamine rises 
    NOT when the reward happens, but BEFORE it, when we predict it's coming.
    This is why you crave a drink at your usual time.
    
  • WITHDRAWAL: When dopamine drops (during alcohol withdrawal), we feel
    anxious, unhappy, and strongly motivated to restore it. Alcohol brings
    dopamine back up, reinforcing the addiction cycle.


=== EMOTIONAL STATES ===

These translate dopamine levels into how you actually FEEL:

  • Happiness (0-10): Reflects current dopamine & reward. High when 
    drinking or anticipating reward.
    
  • Craving (0-10): The urge to drink. Driven by withdrawal (low dopamine)
    and learned anticipation (brain predicting a drink is coming).
    
  • Sleepiness (0-10): Low dopamine + high B-process (withdrawal) makes
    you feel foggy and unmotivated. You might drink to stay awake, or
    the alcohol sedates you deeper.
    
  • Anxiety (0-10): The stress of withdrawal. Grows during time without
    alcohol, peaks just before your usual drink time, then drops when
    you drink.


=== DOPAMINE CONTROLS ===

These adjustable parameters control how the model behaves:

  • Learning Rate (alpha): How quickly the brain learns to predict the
    reward. Higher = faster learning = quicker dependence formation.
    
  • DA Clearance Tau: How fast dopamine is removed from the synapse.
    Longer = dopamine effects last longer = stronger reward feeling.
    
  • Withdrawal DA Suppression (K): How much withdrawal shrinks dopamine.
    Higher = worse withdrawal anxiety and craving.
    
  • Tolerance Step (per drink): How much the brain adapts to each drink.
    Higher = faster tolerance = need more to feel the same.
    
  • A-Process Tau (Fast time constant): Speed of dopamine response to
    alcohol. Reflects how quickly the reward hits.
    
  • B-Process Tau (Slow time constant): Speed of withdrawal buildup.
    Reflects how long it takes to feel the hangover/cravings.
    
  • Dopamine Gains: Overall dopamine responsiveness to alcohol. Higher
    = more dopamine per drink = stronger addiction.


=== GRAPHS ===

  • Dopamine Dynamics: Shows actual dopamine levels (blue), tonic baseline
    (dashed black), and phasic bursts/dips (red).
    
  • Emotional State: Shows how happiness, craving, and anxiety change
    over time. Craving typically spikes right before a scheduled drink.
    
  • Reward Learning: Shows actual reward (alcohol effect), predicted
    reward (brain's expectation), and the prediction error (surprise).
    
  • Drinking Pattern: Shows how craving builds in anticipation of a
    scheduled drink, peaking at drink time.


=== CHRONIC ALCOHOLISM SCENARIO ===

To model someone who drinks at the same time every day:

  1. Go to "Drinking Pattern" panel.
  2. Set "Scheduled Drinking Enabled" to TRUE.
  3. Set "Time of Daily Drink" (in minutes after midnight, e.g., 1080 = 6 PM).
  4. Adjust "Tolerance Step" and "Withdrawal DA Suppression" to increase
     dependence severity.
  5. Run the simulation for several days. Watch:
     - Craving builds in the hours before the scheduled drink.
     - Dopamine spikes when the drink is consumed.
     - Withdrawal anxiety grows after drinking (B-process activation).
     - Brain learns to anticipate the drink; craving appears earlier each day.


=== INTERPRETATION TIPS ===

  • HIGH CRAVING + ANXIETY = The brain is in withdrawal. Dopamine is low.
    The person feels motivated to drink to escape the bad feeling.
    
  • HIGH HAPPINESS AFTER DRINKING = Dopamine reward system reinforcing
    the behavior. This is why drinking feels SO good in the short term.
    
  • ANTICIPATION SPIKE BEFORE DRINK TIME = Learned behavior. The brain
    has learned the time of day and predicts the drink is coming.
    
  • SLOW RECOVERY OF HAPPINESS BETWEEN DRINKS = The B-process (withdrawal)
    is persistent. This is why chronic drinkers feel perpetually anxious
    or depressed without alcohol.

    </text>
    <color> BLACK </color>
  </textbox>

